---
/**
 * Case Studies — side-by-side cards with result metrics.
 * Shows before→after transformation with quantifiable results.
 * Fifth and final beat of the arc, after the Offer. Rewrite lands in RAP-376.
 * Hardcoded content — no props needed.
 */
---

<section id="case-studies" class="relative not-prose scroll-mt-[72px]">
  <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 md:px-6 py-16 md:py-24 lg:py-32">
    <!-- Section header -->
    <div
      class="mb-10 md:mb-14 intersect-once intersect-quarter motion-safe:md:opacity-0 motion-safe:md:intersect:animate-fade"
    >
      <h2 class="font-heading text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-default">Results in the real world</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-muted">Here's what changed for businesses like yours.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Cards -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <!-- Card 1 — Fortune 500 Facility Services -->
      <div
        class="rounded-xl border-l-4 border-l-accent bg-white p-6 md:p-8 intersect-once intersect-quarter motion-safe:md:opacity-0 motion-safe:md:intersect:animate-fade"
      >
        <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-label font-mono text-muted block mb-4">
          Facility Services &middot; Fortune 500
        </span>
        <p class="font-heading text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-primary mb-4">200 &rarr; 20 hrs/month</p>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-muted mb-6">
          Four disconnected systems, no unified view of spend against budget. Every reporting cycle burned 200 hours of
          manual extraction &mdash; and reports were already weeks stale. The disorganized process was putting a
          high-value semiconductor client relationship at risk.
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-default">
          <p>
            <strong>What changed:</strong> We built an automated pipeline that reconciles all four systems continuously,
            surfacing real-time budget vs. actuals by contract section. Leadership now sees where spend is trending mid-cycle
            &mdash; with time to act before billing closes.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-1.5 text-sm text-muted">
          <li>90% reduction in reporting labor</li>
          <li>$6,300&ndash;$7,200 in capacity recovered per month</li>
          <li>Client relationship restored &mdash; contract no longer at risk</li>
          <li>Finance team shifted from data assembly to financial oversight</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 2 — Apparel Redefined -->
      <div
        class="rounded-xl border-l-4 border-l-secondary/30 bg-section p-6 md:p-8 intersect-once intersect-quarter motion-safe:md:opacity-0 motion-safe:md:intersect:animate-fade"
      >
        <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-label font-mono text-muted block mb-4">
          Apparel Redefined &middot; Chicago
        </span>
        <p class="font-heading text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-primary mb-4">Reactive &rarr; proactive</p>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-muted mb-6">
          Orders moved through embroidery, printing, and fulfillment with no consistent metrics. Leadership relied on
          experience and intuition &mdash; every problem was already a crisis by the time it surfaced. There was no way
          to separate a bad week from a bad trend.
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-default">
          <p>
            <strong>What changed:</strong> We built an automated operations dashboard tracking on-time rate, median cycle
            time, and flow efficiency. It became a fixed part of the leadership team's weekly review rhythm.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-1.5 text-sm text-muted">
          <li>Leadership has a reliable weekly picture of operational health</li>
          <li>Changes can now be tested with clear before/after visibility</li>
          <li>Dashboard surfaced the levers that actually drive performance</li>
          <li>Problems visible as trends before they become crises</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
